<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> get a ski boot bag that fits your boots plus your helmet, gloves, and goggles, with a separate boot compartment and drain holes or venting. A backpack-style bag is easiest to carry through airports and parking lots; a duffel-style bag holds more. If your feet get cold, a heated boot bag warms boots before you put them on.</p></div>

<h2>Why a boot bag is worth it</h2>
<p>Ski and snowboard boots are the gear you least want to lose or replace. They're fitted to your feet, they're awkward to carry loose, and on a trip they're the one thing you should never check without a backup plan. A good boot bag keeps boots and small gear together, protects your car and luggage from wet liners, and turns a clumsy armful into one bag on your back.</p>

<h2>Size: what needs to fit</h2>
<p>Start with a list of what you'll carry, then pick a bag with room for all of it:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Boots:</strong> ski boots are bulkier than snowboard boots, especially in larger sizes. Check the bag's listed boot size range if it gives one.</li>
  <li><strong>Helmet:</strong> many bags have a dedicated helmet pocket or a top compartment that fits one.</li>
  <li><strong>Small gear:</strong> gloves, goggles, neck gaiter, socks, and a base layer.</li>
  <li><strong>Extras:</strong> snacks, sunscreen, a spare lens, tools, and your lift pass.</li>
</ul>
<p>A bag that's slightly too big is easier to live with than one you have to wrestle closed every morning.</p>

<h2>Backpack vs duffel</h2>
<h3>Backpack-style boot bags</h3>
<p>These carry on both shoulders with padded straps, leaving your hands free for skis or a board. They're the most comfortable choice for walking from a parking lot or through an airport, and the most common shape for carry-on travel.</p>
<h3>Duffel-style boot bags</h3>
<p>Duffels usually hold more and open wider, which makes packing and unpacking easy in a locker or car. Some have shoulder straps; few carry as comfortably as a true backpack over long distances.</p>
<h3>Which to choose</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fly to ski:</strong> backpack style, sized to your airline's carry-on limits.</li>
  <li><strong>Drive to the hill:</strong> either works; a duffel is nice if you carry a lot of layers.</li>
  <li><strong>Families:</strong> a larger duffel or one bag per person so gear doesn't get mixed up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Features that matter</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Separate boot compartments:</strong> keep wet boots away from dry clothes. Side pockets for each boot are common.</li>
  <li><strong>Drain grommets or mesh vents:</strong> let melted snow drain and liners air out.</li>
  <li><strong>Standing mat:</strong> some bags unzip into a mat so you can change on a dry surface in the parking lot.</li>
  <li><strong>Tough, water-resistant base:</strong> bags get set down in slush and snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Fleece-lined goggle pocket:</strong> protects lenses from scratches.</li>
  <li><strong>Grab handles and luggage pass-through:</strong> useful in airports and on rolling bags.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Heated boot bags</h2>
<p>Heated boot bags have a built-in heater that warms your boots before you put them on, powered by a car outlet, a wall outlet, or a battery depending on the model. Warm boots are easier to buckle and more comfortable for the first runs, especially if you change in a cold parking lot or your feet get cold easily. The trade-offs are weight, cost, and needing power. If you mostly change in a heated lodge, a regular bag is fine.</p>

<h2>Flying with ski boots</h2>
<p>Many skiers carry boots onto the plane, because boots fitted to your feet are hard to replace if luggage goes missing, while skis and snowboards can be rented. Before you fly:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Check your airline's carry-on size and weight limits, and measure your packed bag.</li>
  <li>Some airlines allow a boot bag as a carry-on or count it with checked ski gear; rules vary, so confirm with your airline.</li>
  <li>Pack your helmet and goggles in the boot bag too, so the essentials travel with you.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the rest of your gear list, see the <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %>.</p>

<h2>Packing tips</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Put boots in their compartments buckled loosely so they keep their shape.</li>
  <li>Stuff socks and gloves inside the boots to save space.</li>
  <li>Keep goggles in their own soft pocket, away from buckles.</li>
  <li>Dry liners overnight, not in the closed bag. Leave the bag open to air out between trips.</li>
  <li>Keep a small kit in an outside pocket: lip balm, sunscreen, hand warmers, and a snack.</li>
</ol>

